module test.claim.by_ref_bridge_argument_declared_carrier_witness

// THE BORROW SITE DECLARES A CALL ARGUMENT'S CARRIER.
//
// A by-ref runtime bridge (`extdeps.languages.rust.emit rt_function_registry` rows with
// `passes_by_ref: true` — sorted_map_keys, map_keys, map_values, lookup, the string family) took its
// argument as `&<expr>`. When `<expr>` is a call to a GENERIC declaration, rustc receives that borrow
// as the only evidence for the call's own type arguments and uses it: `fold_list`'s accumulator is
// pinned by the borrowed parameter instead of by its own `empty:` argument, so the emitted crate
// refuses a program the .dag checker had accepted. That is
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode by_ref_primitive_over_a_generic_call_argument_infers_from_the_borrow,
// first measured on #15164 at 47612d0cb20d (v2-bootstrap run 40968160998, in
// v2.compiler.occurrence_resolution_census).
//
// THE FORM, EXACTLY. `rust_by_ref_bridge_argument` emits
// `v1_rt::sorted_map_keys({ let __by_ref_arg: &<carrier> = &<expr>; __by_ref_arg })`. The binding is a
// REFERENCE to the carrier, not the carrier itself, because `render_node_type` spells the modelled type
// while the emitted expression may carry it behind a pointer (`Rc<im::HashMap<..>>` for a model
// `Map<String, Bool>`): a typed `let` is a coercion site, so `&<expr>` reaches `&<carrier>` by deref
// coercion exactly as the plain borrow did at the call, and the annotation still names the carrier,
// which is the only reason the binding exists. An earlier revision bound the value itself and the seed
// rebuild of run 41299024017 said so: `expected im::HashMap<_, _>, found Rc<im::HashMap<_, bool>>` in
// the `std.types` mirror. The same failure made a second rule necessary: an annotation whose type still
// has an unresolved variable below a resolved outer node pins nothing -- rustc unifies the hole with the
// initializer -- so `rust_type_node_is_fully_resolved` walks the inferred NODE and `rust_node_carrier_decl`
// writes no annotation in that case, leaving the plain borrow. It reads the tree the checker built rather
// than the renderer's text, because `(_, i64)`, `Fn(_) -> i64` and `Vec<(i64, _)>` are all holes that no
// substring set would catch, and a text test would silently follow whatever spacing the renderer uses
// (DESIGN.md §4: the richer source exists, so a heuristic is not permitted).
//
// WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT CLAIM ABOUT THE GUARD. `rust_type_node_is_fully_resolved` has no red input
// reachable from these arms: the programs below are fully resolved, so they emit the same annotation with
// or without the guard, and a claim over them could only ever be green -- a control that cannot fail
// controls nothing (§5: "a real consumer green by execution plus a discriminating input that goes red
// when the behavior is wrong"). The guard's red input is the seed itself, which is where a hole reached a
// by-ref argument: the rebuild of run 41299024017 (`expected im::HashMap<_, _>, found
// Rc<im::HashMap<_, bool>>`), and its rerun on this head is the executing evidence. Stating that here is
// the honest form of the claim; a fixture invented to look like it would have the checker accept
// something it should refuse.
//
// THE REPAIR IS AT THE BORROW, NOT AT THE NAME, AND THESE CLAIMS PIN THAT. In
// `src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag`, `rust_by_ref_bridge_argument` now binds a CALL argument at the carrier
// `Node.inferred` already resolved for it — the type v1 derived, rendered by `render_node_type`, the
// same authority every other type position in the emitted program goes through — and borrows the
// binding:
// `v1_rt::sorted_map_keys({ let __by_ref_arg: &Rc<HashMap<i64, bool>> = &fold_list(..); __by_ref_arg })`.
// Claim one asserts that form and forbids the old one, so deleting the rule reddens it twice over:
// the required text disappears and the forbidden text returns.
//
// WHY THE SECOND CLAIM EXISTS. A name, a field or an index is already a place of a known carrier;
// rustc has no type arguments to invent there, so wrapping it in a block would be redundant work
// (DESIGN section 2) at each of the ~360 plain by-ref sites already in the committed mirrors. The
// second claim asserts the plain borrow survives for a name, so widening the rule into a wrapper at
// every borrow site reddens the floor too.
//
// WHAT THESE CLAIMS ARE, EXACTLY. Two arms over two programs, each one seed compile: the call arm pins the
// emitted form, and the name arm pins that the plain borrow survives where no binding is needed, so widening
// the rule into a wrapper at every borrow site reddens the floor. There is no third, rustc-level artifact
// behind them: an earlier revision pointed at `fixtures/fixture_closure_rustc/
// by_ref_primitive_generic_call_argument_probe.dag` and `by_ref_primitive_bound_argument_probe.dag` as the
// differential, and neither file exists in this repository -- citations to a home that does not resolve are
// §3 defects, so they are gone rather than left as decoration. The rustc-level fact is the seed rebuild
// itself (recurring_failure_mode
// by_ref_primitive_over_a_generic_call_argument_infers_from_the_borrow, run 41299024017).
//
// The programs below are kept in the exact spelling that was measured to emit the defect form, so a
// reader can run either one through `gunbc compile` and diff it against the assertion.

// A by-ref bridge over an unbound call to the generic `fold_list`: the shape that was measured emitting the
// defect form, kept in the spelling it was measured in so a reader can run it through `gunbc compile` and
// diff the result against the assertion below.
fn by_ref_generic_call_argument_source() -> String {
  "module byrefgenericarg\n\nimport std.algebra { fold_list, empty_map, map_insert }\nimport std.types { Bool, Int, List, Map }\n\nfn keyed_ids(ids: List<Int>) -> List<Int> {\n  sorted_map_keys(fold_list(xs: ids, empty: empty_map(), cons: fn(m, id) { map_insert(m: m, key: id, value: true) }))\n}\n"
}

// The same bridge over a NAME: the shape that has always compiled and must keep its plain borrow.
fn by_ref_plain_name_source() -> String {
  "module byrefplainname\n\nimport std.types { Int, String }\n\nfn use_len(s: String) -> Int {\n  string_length(s: s)\n}\n"
}

test fn by_ref_bridge_over_a_call_argument_is_bound_at_its_declared_carrier() -> Bool {
  compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    by_ref_generic_call_argument_source(),
    "src/byrefgenericarg.rs",
    ["v1_rt::sorted_map_keys({ let __by_ref_arg: &", "; __by_ref_arg }"],
    ["v1_rt::sorted_map_keys(&crate::std_algebra::fold_list("]
  )
}

test fn by_ref_bridge_over_a_name_keeps_the_plain_borrow() -> Bool {
  compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    by_ref_plain_name_source(),
    "src/byrefplainname.rs",
    ["v1_rt::string_length(&s"],
    ["v1_rt::string_length(&{"]
  )
}
